 ## Overview
-SparSplat is a generalizable, feed-forward model that maps a set of sparse input images to 2D Gaussian Splatting (2DGS) parameters in a single forward pass to perform both 3D shape reconstruction and novel view synthesis [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [MVSGaussian](12847-anysplat-pipeline.md) by using 2D primitives instead of 3D Gaussians, which ensures multi-view depth consistency required for coherent TSDF mesh fusion, a task prior generalizable GS methods failed to support [S1, unofficial]. Unlike implicit methods such as [SparseNeuS](15257-finesplat.md) or [UfoRecon](12847-anysplat-pipeline.md), SparSplat offers superior novel view synthesis capabilities alongside reconstruction, with inference speeds nearly two orders of magnitude faster [S1, unofficial].
+SparSplat is a generalizable, feed-forward model that maps a set of sparse input images (typically 3 views) to a pixel-aligned map of 2D Gaussian Splatting (2DGS) parameters for a target view, enabling both novel view synthesis and explicit 3D surface reconstruction [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [MVSGaussian](12847-anysplat-pipeline.md) by using 2D planar primitives instead of 3D volumetric primitives, which ensures multi-view depth consistency required for coherent TSDF mesh fusion [S1, unofficial]. Unlike prior generalizable Gaussian methods designed primarily for novel view synthesis, SparSplat is explicitly optimized for feed-forward surface reconstruction, achieving state-of-the-art Chamfer distance on the DTU benchmark while offering inference speeds nearly two orders of magnitude faster than implicit neural representations [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A set of $N$ RGB images $\{I_i\}_{i=1}^N$ where $I_i \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 3}$, and their associated camera poses $P_i$ (intrinsics and extrinsics). The model is trained with $N=3$ and image resolution $640 \times 512$ [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Outputs:** For a given target view $P_t$, the model $\Phi$ predicts a pixel-aligned 2DGS Gaussian parameter map $O_t = \{s, q, p, \alpha, c\} \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times (2+4+3+1+3)}$, where $s$ is scale (2D), $q$ is rotation (quaternion, 4D), $p$ is centroid position (3D), $\alpha$ is opacity (1D), and $c$ is color (3D) [S1, unofficial]. Additionally, an explicit 3D triangle mesh $S$ can be extracted via TSDF fusion of the splatted depths [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A set of $N$ color images $\{I_i\}_{i=1}^N$ (where $N=3$) and their corresponding camera poses $\{P_i\}_{i=1}^N$. Each image $I_i \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 3}$.
+*   **Output:** For a target view with pose $P_t$, the model $\Phi$ predicts a Gaussian parameter map $O_t \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times W \times 13}$, where the 13 channels correspond to the 2DGS attributes: scale ($s$, 2), rotation quaternion ($q$, 4), 3D position ($p$, 3), opacity ($\alpha$, 1), and color ($c$, 3) [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-The core data structure is the parameter map $O_t$. Each pixel $(u,v)$ in the target view holds a vector of 13 floats:
-1.  Scale $s \in \mathbb{R}^2$
-2.  Rotation $q \in \mathbb{R}^4$ (normalized quaternion)
-3.  Position $p \in \mathbb{R}^3$
-4.  Opacity $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}^1$
-5.  Color $c \in \mathbb{R}^3$
+The core data structure is the parameter map $O_t$. For each pixel $(u, v)$ in the target image, the map stores:
+1.  $s \in \mathbb{R}^2$: Scale parameters (width and height of the 2D Gaussian ellipse).
+2.  $q \in \mathbb{R}^4$: Rotation quaternion defining the orientation of the 2D plane in 3D space.
+3.  $p \in \mathbb{R}^3$: 3D centroid position of the Gaussian primitive.
+4.  $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}$: Opacity.
+5.  $c \in \mathbb{R}^3$: RGB color.
 
